# The prelude: every binary operator, as an ordinary function bound to its
# symbol, and its fixity. It is in scope everywhere, modules included.
#
# A fixity declaration gives operators a precedence (0, loosest, to 9) and an
# associativity: infixl (left), infixr (right) or infix (none). An operator
# without one is infixl 9. Application binds tighter than any operator, and
# prefix - and ! bind tighter than * but looser than ^.
#
# Operators are functions: (+) is the function, (+ 1) and (1 +) are sections,
# and a + b = ... or (+) = ... defines one. Overloading an existing operator
# with typed clauses is fine; its clauses are tried first, then these:
#     (Money a) + (Money b) = Money (a + b)
# Defining new operators is possible but not recommended: a named function is
# easier to read and to search for.
infixr 0 $
infixl 2 ||
infixl 3 &&
infixl 4 == != < > <= >=
infixr 5 :
infixl 6 + - ++
infixl 7 * / %
infixr 8 ^
infixr 9 \

($) = primApply
(||) = primOr
(&&) = primAnd
(==) = primEq
(!=) = primNe
(<) = primLt
(>) = primGt
(<=) = primLe
(>=) = primGe
(:) = primCons
(+) = primAdd
(-) = primSub
(++) = primAppend
(*) = primMul
(/) = primDiv
(%) = primMod
(^) = primPow
(\) = primCompose
